course: dictionary
lesson: 1952
title: One word, many jobs
level: primary-6
stars: 1
explain: Communist can work as a noun or an adjective. As a noun, a communist is a person who believes in communism. As an adjective, it describes things, like a communist country or communist rule. Look at the words around it to see which job it is doing.
explain: Communism is an idea: land, factories and wealth should be owned by everyone together, not by private people, so that people are more equal. In «The Soviet Union, the communist state that grew out of the Russian Empire», communist describes the state, so it is an adjective.
explain: A capital letter often means a named group. «The Communist Party» and «the Communists» are names of a political party. Mei and her teacher use the capital this way when they argue about who fought Japan.
explain: Context gives clues, but people also disagree about communism. Supporters praise its aim of equality, while critics point to one-party rule and a lack of freedom. A good reader notices both views and does not decide from one word alone.
fact: A communist is a person who believes in communism.
fact: Communism is the idea that land, factories and wealth should be owned by everyone together, so that people are more equal.
fact: As an adjective, communist describes a country, party or government that follows these ideas, like the old Soviet Union or China today.
fact: A capital letter, as in «the Communist Party», often shows a named group.
fact: The Cold War was a conflict between communist and capitalist countries.
exercise: «The Soviet Union, the communist state that grew out of the Russian Empire, broke up in 1991.» In this sentence, what kind of word is communist? | A name for a group of people | A verb, an action word | A noun, the name of a person | *An adjective, describing the state
exercise: Mei says only the Communists fought Japan. What does the teacher say? | Only the Nationalists fought Japan | *Both the Nationalists and the Communists fought Japan | Nobody fought Japan
exercise: What does the capital letter in «the Communist Party» tell you? | It means the word is a mistake | It means the word is very important | *It names a particular group, a political party | It shows that the word starts a question
test: «Communist rule» means | a rule about the weather | *a government run by one party that follows communist ideas | a country that never has any laws | a rule that everyone must wear the same colour
test: Which of these is the best summary of communism? | A way of making everyone rich by lending money | A system where people choose their own leaders in every village | A type of sports club | *The idea that land, factories and wealth should be owned by everyone together
exam: Read: «Both the Nationalist government of Chiang Kai-shek and the Communists fought Japan.» What does Communists mean here, and how do you know? | A type of weather, because it is cold | A person's name, because it has a capital letter at the start of a sentence | *A political group, because of the capital letter and the word fought alongside the Nationalists | A kind of food, because it is in a list
